 "text": "Deep research agents search the web for minutes, then hand back a long, cited report. So how do you grade one? Most benchmarks need experts to write every task, score every report on one fixed rubric, and only check the facts that come with a citation. A new paper, DeepResearchEval, automates all three. First, the tasks. Starting from ten domains, a model invents fifty personas: people with real jobs and real questions. Each one asks for four research tasks. Then come two filters. One keeps only tasks that truly need recent, multi-source digging. The other lets a model try each task without the web. If it does well anyway, the task is cut. A hundred and fifty-five survive. Next, quality. Every report is judged on four general dimensions, like coverage and insight. Then the judge reads the task, and adds dimensions just for it, like metric utility for a policy comparison. Each dimension gets a weight and its own criteria, scored one to ten, and they all roll up into one score. Then, facts. An agent splits the report into segments, and pulls out every checkable statement: numbers, dates, names. It searches the web for each one, cited or not, and labels it right, wrong, or unknown. The share that's right is the factual ratio. The authors tested nine deep research systems. On quality, Gemini 2.5 Pro led, at eight point five out of ten. But for every system, the task-specific score was the lowest. The reports read well, but often miss what that particular task needed. On facts, Manus led, with eighty-two percent of its statements right. Outright errors were rare. Most of the risk was unknowns: claims the evidence couldn't confirm either way. Can the judges be trusted? A second judge kept seven of the nine systems in the same place, and the fact checker agreed with human experts on seventy-three percent of statements. And because the tasks are generated, not hand-written, the benchmark can stay live, with fresh tasks whenever they're needed.",
